1
00:00:01,180 --> 00:00:03,120
Did Apollo 9 almost fail?

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:04,380
You may never have heard of it,

3
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:06,850
but this ten‑day mission in March 1969

4
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:10,240
was the first flight of the full Apollo spacecraft

5
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:11,840
— command module,

6
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:12,780
service module,

7
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:15,180
and lunar module flying together as one.

8
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:19,520
A single engine misfire nearly derailed the entire Moon program.

9
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:21,780
Here is what actually happened,

10
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:22,920
from NASA’s own records.

11
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:28,520
Three men flew Apollo 9. Commander James A. McDivitt,

12
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:32,160
a Gemini 4 veteran and commander of Apollo 9,

13
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:34,280
first flight of the complete set of Apollo

14
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:37,340
hardware and first flight of the Lunar Module.

15
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,620
Command Module Pilot David R. Scott,

16
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:43,120
who flew Gemini 8 and Apollo 9,

17
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:45,480
later commanded Apollo 15,

18
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:49,480
and logged 546 hours and 54 minutes in space

19
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:53,480
including 20 hours 46 minutes of EVA; he later became

20
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:56,490
Director of NASA’s Dryden Flight Research Center.

21
00:00:57,240 --> 00:00:59,800
Lunar Module Pilot Russell L. Schweickart,

22
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:03,150
from NASA’s third astronaut group in October 1963,

23
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:09,080
logged 241 hours in space on Apollo 9 — the first manned flight of

24
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:12,680
the Lunar Module — and during EVA tested the portable life support

25
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,830
system backpack subsequently used on lunar surface explorations.

26
00:01:17,580 --> 00:01:21,540
Their backup crew: Charles Conrad Jr. as commander,

27
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,010
Alan L. Bean as Lunar Module Pilot,

28
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:27,280
Richard F. Gordon Jr. as Command Module Pilot.

29
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,760
The two spacecraft had names: the Lunar Module,

30
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:32,440
nicknamed Spider (LM‑3),

31
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:34,740
and the Command and Service Module,

32
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:37,110
nicknamed Gumdrop (CSM‑104).

33
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:41,280
This was the first manned flight of the LM — the first

34
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:43,760
manned flight of all lunar hardware in Earth orbit.

35
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:47,250
Two hours and 41 minutes after launch,

36
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:49,320
CSM Gumdrop turned to face Spider,

37
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:52,270
still attached to the S‑IVB third stage,

38
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,740
and completed the first successful docking of the Apollo program.

39
00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:57,430
About an hour later,

40
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:01,400
springs ejected the docked spacecraft from the S‑IVB. The

41
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,370
crew pressurized the tunnel between Gumdrop and Spider,

42
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:06,340
removed the CM hatch,

43
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:08,420
connected umbilicals to power the LM

44
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:10,240
while the two spacecraft remained docked,

45
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:11,700
then replaced the hatch.

46
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:14,120
On flight day 3,

47
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:17,540
McDivitt and Schweickart put on spacesuits and transferred to the

48
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:21,060
LM through the tunnel connection to perform a systems checkout.

49
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:26,200
This included a 367‑second firing of the LM descent engine

50
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:29,220
to simulate the throttle pattern to be used during a lunar

51
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:33,470
landing mission — McDivitt controlled the final 59 seconds,

52
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:37,080
varying the thrust from 10 to 40 percent and shutting it off

53
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:40,440
manually — the first crewed throttling of an engine in space.

54
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,300
They also tested the landing and rendezvous radars and

55
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:48,230
made a 5‑minute television broadcast from inside Spider.

56
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:51,260
The flight plan called for a two‑hour EVA to simulate

57
00:02:51,260 --> 00:02:54,240
external transfer rescue techniques — the only

58
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:57,810
planned EVA from the LM before an actual lunar landing.

59
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:00,710
But Schweickart suffered space motion sickness,

60
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:02,840
vomiting twice during the first two days,

61
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:05,580
so the EVA was delayed and then shortened

62
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:09,270
from a planned 2 hours to 37.5 minutes.

63
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:10,040
Instead,

64
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:14,220
he climbed out of the LM porch for a 37.5‑minute EVA,

65
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:16,880
testing the EVA mobility unit including

66
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:19,140
the portable life support system backpack

67
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:22,880
— this was the first and only EVA before the lunar landing

68
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:26,440
and the only opportunity to test the PLSS in space.

69
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:29,800
Schweickart floated out the LM side hatch onto the front porch

70
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:33,820
using gold‑painted foot restraints ("golden slippers"); Scott

71
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:37,060
opened the CM side hatch and floated partway out (stand‑up

72
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:40,280
EVA); both spent about 37 minutes outside.

73
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:44,890
Schweickart’s only connection to the LM was a 25‑foot nylon rope;

74
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:47,820
he maneuvered on handrails near the hatch (found them easy),

75
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:49,440
took photographs,

76
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:52,360
and described rain squalls over KSC. Scott

77
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:55,220
performed a stand‑up EVA (about 1 hour 4 minutes)

78
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:57,880
from the CM hatch without a PLSS,

79
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:00,400
connected to CM life support via

80
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:02,140
umbilicals and a Pressure Control Valve,

81
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:03,700
and filmed Schweickart.

82
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:06,900
Scott stood in the open side hatch of Gumdrop

83
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,860
observing Schweickart’s EVA activities.

84
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,960
Mission Control called it a complete success — the first

85
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:16,940
and only spacesuit/PLSS test before the Moon landing.

86
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:18,580
Flight day 5:

87
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:21,980
McDivitt and Schweickart undocked in Spider for a 6 hour

88
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:25,280
23‑minute independent flight while Scott remained in Gumdrop.

89
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:28,390
About 45 minutes after undocking,

90
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:33,840
Spider’s DPS engine fired 19 seconds (first at 10% thrust then throttled

91
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:38,480
to 40%) beginning separation and placing it about 50 miles from

92
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:43,820
Gumdrop; a 22‑second DPS burn opened the distance to about 100 miles.

93
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:47,500
To begin rendezvous Spider fired ascent‑stage RCS

94
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:50,960
thrusters for 32 seconds while jettisoning the Descent

95
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:54,520
Stage (which remained in orbit until March 22,

96
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:59,760
burning up over the Indian Ocean); first APS burn lasted 3 seconds,

97
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:04,740
second APS burn 38 seconds put Spider on an intercept course with

98
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:08,980
Gumdrop; two small course corrections refined the trajectory.

99
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:11,780
Spider stopped about 100 feet from Gumdrop for

100
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:14,420
a pitchover maneuver; Scott photographed the

101
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:17,580
ascent stage including its engine ("the biggest,

102
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:18,040
friendliest,

103
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:22,520
funniest looking Spider I've ever seen"); McDivitt brought Spider in

104
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:26,840
for smooth docking; the craft flew separately for 6 hours 23 minutes.

105
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:31,240
Two hours after docking McDivitt and Schweickart rejoined Scott

106
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:35,120
in Gumdrop and shortly after jettisoned Spider; Mission

107
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:39,640
Control commanded Spider's APS to fire for six minutes into a

108
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:43,320
highly elliptical Earth orbit from which it did not decay until

109
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:49,240
October 23 1981. Apollo 9 was the first manned flight of the

110
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:53,220
LM; first manned flight of all lunar hardware in Earth orbit.

111
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:57,620
The mission demonstrated docked and undocked maneuvers,

112
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:59,760
performed a lunar module–command module rendezvous,

113
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:02,680
and demonstrated extra‑vehicular capability

114
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:06,670
— everything Apollo 11 would need to land on the Moon.

115
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:09,400
The backpack Schweickart tested in space was

116
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:12,530
subsequently used on the lunar surface explorations.

117
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:14,720
Every system that would later carry

118
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:16,830
humans to the Moon had been flown,

119
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:17,860
tested,

120
00:06:17,860 --> 00:06:19,220
and proven in Earth orbit.

121
00:06:20,440 --> 00:06:22,540
Every claim in this video comes from NASA primary

122
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:26,780
sources: the NASA Apollo 9 Mission Details page,

123
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:28,130
NASA mission pages,

124
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:30,000
NASA history articles,

125
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:33,700
the Lunar and Planetary Institute Apollo 9 mission overview,

126
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:34,920
the Apollo Flight Journal,

127
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:37,100
and the Apollo 9 Mission Report

128
00:06:37,940 --> 00:06:40,100
(MSC‑PA‑R‑69‑2,

129
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:46,320
May 1969). All are U.S. government works in the public domain; NASA

130
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:53,300
logos and insignia are restricted by law (14 CFR 1221). If you see

131
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:57,080
a claim about Apollo 9 that doesn't trace back to a primary source,

132
00:06:57,380 --> 00:06:58,480
ask to see the document.

133
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:00,820
That's how you know what really happened.

134
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:04,680
Apollo 9: the mission that proved the Lunar Module

135
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:07,000
could fly — and cleared the way for the Moon.

